issue.body:
There is a memory leak in Cake2.x when using an SMTP transport with CakeEmail.

Turns out this is a bug that was present in Cake3.x as well (see issue #6128) and is already solved by @lorenzo for that branch. The issue and the fix it's the same for Cake2.x.

Each email sent increases the memory foot-print, with or without attachments, until memory limit is reached and the process is killed. This makes impossible to create a deamon with CakeShell for sending emails.

You can replicate the bug with the following shell:

``` php
<?php

App::uses('CakeEmail', 'Network/Email');

class EmailMemoryLeakShell extends Shell
{
    public function main()
    {
        $n = 1;
        while ($n < 100) {
            $email = new CakeEmail('smtp');
            $email->from(array('noreply@incrementacrm.com' => 'Ventas'));
            $email->to('your@email.com');
            $email->subject('Testing subject');
            // 3 Mb attachment to make it more obvious.
            $email->attachments(array('/Users/linnk/Desktop/calls.xlsx'));
            $email->send('This is a testing message for SMTP config.');

            $this->out('CakeEmail instance #'.$n);
            $n++;
        }
        while (true) {
            $this->out('Total CakeEmail instances: '.$n);
            sleep(1);
            // Continue so you can see if the garbage collector kicks in.
            continue;
        }
    }
}
```

I run the Cake Shell using PM2: `pm2 start app/Console/cake.php -- -working "app/" email_memory_leak`. This way I can keep an eye on the PM2 monitor (`pm2 monit`) and see if the actual memory foot-print decreases.

I forked the repository and fix the bug the same way is fixed in the previous issue. I will submit my pull request with the fix if that helps.
